          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is osteoarthritis, and is it the same thing as arthritis?",
          "answer": "The search results do not include a plain definition of osteoarthritis or of arthritis as a general term, so I can't fully answer the \"same thing\" part from the material I have.\n\nWhat the sources do show is how the condition is classified. In the ICD-10-CM coding system (Chapter 13, musculoskeletal conditions, source: US clinical modification of ICD-10), osteoarthritis has its own block of codes, M15 to M19. Those codes are sorted first by joint and then by cause and side of the body. Rheumatoid arthritis and gout are classified separately, in M05 to M14, which covers inflammatory joint conditions. Infectious joint conditions and autoinflammatory syndromes have their own blocks too. So in this classification, osteoarthritis is one distinct type of joint disease among several, not a label for all of them.\n\nThe clinical reference (condition summary on osteoarthritis) says that a hot, swollen, very painful joint with fever is not a typical osteoarthritis flare. It suggests infection or crystal arthritis and needs urgent assessment. Prolonged morning stiffness, several small joints affected on both sides of the body, or whole-body symptoms suggest inflammatory arthritis instead. Sudden locking or giving way of a joint, or new nerve symptoms with back or neck pain, also call for prompt assessment.\n\nIf you want to know more about how osteoarthritis is usually managed or what its classification codes mean, I can cover those next. For a personal question about your own joints, a doctor or other clinician is the right person to ask.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
